      "message": "[Gerrit AI Review] ❌ **Changes requested** — See inline comments for details. [[trajectory](go/traj/df193688-11f7-457c-bdc5-afbf43f7eab1)]\n\n**Review Summary**\n* **Functionality:** The patch correctly blocks legacy v0 template commits when fingerprint authentication is enabled by returning `EC_RES_ACCESS_DENIED`. This successfully forces the use of the authenticated v1 channel.\n* **Security:** This introduces a solid security invariant that prevents arbitrary template injection via unauthenticated paths when the device is in a secure state.\n* **Style:** The code style is clean, and the inline comment clearly explains the security rationale.\n* **Testing \u0026 Test Coverage:** The new Zephyr test (`test_fp_template_v0_disabled_under_auth`) nicely verifies the v0 blocking behavior. However, the migration of legacy tests (`test_fp_command_commit_v3` and `test_fp_command_commit_trivial_salt`) to `EC_CMD_FP_TEMPLATE` version 1 introduces a testing gap. Because `FP_TEMPLATE_DECRYPT` in v1 operates asynchronously, asserting that it returns `EC_RES_SUCCESS` only verifies that the decryption task was successfully queued, completely missing the actual format validation inside `fp_commit_template`. I have added inline comments with two possible ways to fix this.\n* **CL Size \u0026 Scope:** The CL is appropriately sized and focuses tightly on a single security enhancement.\n* **Conclusion:** The core functional and security changes are excellent, but the test coverage regression needs to be addressed before this can be approved.\n\nTAG\u003dagy\nCONV\u003ddf193688-11f7-457c-bdc5-afbf43f7eab1",
